# Assignment 4 Solutions

## Q2.10

### (a) Five examples from the filtered dataset

All five examples were retained after applying language-ID (English, ≥0.65 confidence), Gopher quality filter, NSFW, and toxic-speech classifiers, with PII masking applied to survivors.

---

**Example 1** — `https://actoragecheck.com/actor/Ju-Jin-mo/945428` (1,357 words)

> *Ju Jin-mo Acting Credits — ActorAgeCheck*
> *"ActorAgeCheck works best with JavaScript enabled…"*
> *"Ju Jin-mo was 67 in The Match as 'Gyu-sang'. Movie Released: Wed, Mar 26 2025…"*

A filmography database page listing every movie the actor appeared in, with age-at-release metadata for each. The text is repetitive and template-generated ("Ju Jin-mo was X years old in Y"), but at least factual and coherent. It passes Gopher because alpha-ratio ≈ 0.95 and word count is high. For C4 100-domains LM training this is borderline: the repetitive sentence structure adds very little linguistic variety and the page is dominated by UI chrome (nav menus, filter controls). **Marginally useful at best.**

---

**Example 2** — `https://m.openjurist.org/518/f2d/1184` (6,320 words)

> *518 F.2d 1184 — Brockway v. Department of the Air Force, 1975*
> *"On this appeal by the Department of the Air Force we are required to review a district court order requiring the Air Force to make public all of a safety report on…"*

A full published U.S. federal circuit court opinion. The body of the opinion is dense, well-edited English legal prose arguing statutory interpretation under the Freedom of Information Act. This is exactly the kind of document C4 contains (legal/gov domains are heavily represented). **Excellent for LM training.**

---

**Example 3** — `https://openstock.co/collections/dewalt-accessories` (549 words)

> *"DEWALT ACCESSORIES – Openstock … WELCOME TO OPENSTOCK … Need help? call us +1 |||PHONE_NUMBER|||"*

An e-commerce category listing page for power-tool accessories. The phone number was correctly masked. The majority of the text is navigation chrome (brand names, product categories) repeated twice due to a mobile/desktop menu duplication. There is almost no prose; just lists of product names. **Not useful for LM training — noise with no linguistic content.**

---

**Example 4** — `https://www.hopewell.k12.va.us/apps/events/2022/5/17/10463507/?id=5` (128 words)

> *"Soccer Senior Night | Hopewell City Public Schools … What: Soccer Senior Night. When: 5/17/2022, 7:15 PM – 7:30 PM"*

A school calendar event page. Nearly the entire text is navigation menu items (school names, link labels); the actual content is one event name and a 15-minute time slot. Passes Gopher only because the alpha-word count (112) clears the 50-word floor, but 88% of the words are navigation boilerplate. **Useless for LM training; the Gopher 50-word minimum is too permissive.**

---

**Example 5** — `https://m.openjurist.org/518/f2d/1184` (via idx=3118 — full opinion already cited above); replacing with `https://mindworks.org/blog/testimonials/elke-lackey/` (410 words)

> *"Why More Meditators Are Choosing Mindworks … Elke Lackey … 'The first morning after my introductory lesson, I noticed something different about myself…'"*

A user testimonial on a meditation course website. The actual testimonial quote is one genuine first-person paragraph (~80 words); the rest is navigation and promotional headings. The signal-to-noise ratio is low. For C4's health/wellness domain coverage this is marginally relevant, but the testimonial itself is too short. **Borderline — the prose fragment is fine, but most words are boilerplate.**

---

**Overall assessment:** Only 1 of 5 examples (the federal court opinion) would be considered high-quality C4-style training text. The other four pass our filters but are navigation-heavy, repetitive, or trivially short. The root cause is that Gopher's 50-word alpha floor and 80% alpha-ratio are both too permissive for detecting UI-chrome-dominated pages.

---

### (b) Five examples removed by the pipeline

---

**Example R1** — `http://1kb.klimtoren.be/2016/06/blog-post_30.html` — **removed by language filter** (Dutch, confidence=0.93)

> *"1KB - Juf Marijke … Hoera voor Leonie! 4 Jaar :-) Gepost door juf marijke op 09:22 …"*

A Belgian primary-school teacher's blog post in Dutch celebrating a child's birthday. Legitimately non-English; the language filter correctly excludes it. **Removal justified** — our goal is C4 100 domains which is English-only.

---

**Example R2** — `http://3diasdemarzo.blogspot.com/2005/04/el-gobierno-no-entregar...` — **removed by language filter** (Spanish, confidence=0.97)

> *"El Gobierno no entregará la transcripción de la conversación entre Huarte y Bensmail porque afecta al derecho a la intimidad del prisionero…"*

A Spanish-language political blog covering the 2004 Madrid bombings investigation. The text is substantive investigative journalism. **Removal justified for our English-only goal**, but this would be high-quality training data for a Spanish LM.

---

**Example R3** — `http://allsortsofbooks.blogspot.com/2013/08/the-language-of-sparrows-by-rachel.html` — **removed by Gopher filter** (alpha_ratio = 943/1288 = 0.73 < 0.80)

> *"Inside the mind of a Bibliophile: The Language Of Sparrows by Rachel Phifer … Friday, August 9, 2013 … The Language Of Sparrows … Rachel Phifer … blog archive links …"*

A book review blog. The raw document has extensive Blogger navigation links (archive months, "About Me", sharing buttons) which inflate non-alpha token count. The actual review paragraphs would be useful content. **Removal is borderline** — the signal content is reasonable quality, but the embedded UI chrome justifies rejection. Better boilerplate stripping would have saved this document.

---

**Example R4** — `http://archive.library.nau.edu/digital/collection/cpa/id/116929/` — **removed by Gopher filter** (alpha_words = 13 < 50)

> *"CONTENTdm\nJavascript Required\nTo experience full interactivity, please enable Javascript in your browser."*

A JavaScript-fallback stub page from a university digital archive. The actual content failed to render; the WET extractor captured only the no-JS placeholder. **Removal fully justified.**

---

**Example R5** — `http://javhmm.com/category/38/ass-lover` — **removed by NSFW classifier** (score=0.93)

> *"Watch Ass Lover Jav Online Page 1 | Japanese Adult Video — JAVHMM.COM …"*

An adult video category listing page. The NSFW classifier correctly identifies it. **Removal fully justified.**

---

### (c) Pipeline iterations and changes

Examining the five kept examples above revealed two systematic problems:

**1. Gopher's 50-word floor is too permissive.** The school event page (Example 4, 128 words) and many similar calendar/directory pages pass because they have just enough alpha words. C4 itself filters to documents where the longest sentence alone is ≥ 80 words; a minimum of **200 alpha words** would discard such stubs without harming substantive pages.

**2. Navigation boilerplate inflates word counts.** Examples 3 and 5 pass Gopher because repeated nav-menu text (brand names, link labels) counts as high-alpha-ratio content. A simple within-document exact-line deduplication step (before applying Gopher) removes repeated nav blocks and makes the remaining counts more representative of actual prose.

**Changes made to the pipeline:**

- Raised the effective minimum from 50 to 200 alpha words by adding a `min_alpha_words` parameter to `gopher_quality_filter` (defaulting to 50 for backwards compatibility; the pipeline uses 200 via `--min-alpha-words 200` flag).
- Kept the quality classifier disabled by default (it was trained on a single Wikipedia article and rejected 99.8% of otherwise-passing English documents).
- Added a note in `filter_wet_files.py` for a post-processing dedup pass (`--dedup` flag) to remove repeated lines/documents after all WET files have been processed.

## Q2.11 — Tokenization

**Script:** `tokenize_filtered_data.py`

Reads one or more filtered JSONL files (produced by `filter_wet_files.py`), extracts the `"text"` field from each JSON line, tokenizes with the GPT-2 tokenizer (`AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("gpt2")`), appends the EOS token (`<|endoftext|>`, id=50256) after each document, then saves the concatenated IDs as a `numpy.uint16` binary file via `ids_array.tofile(output_path)`.

Tokenization is parallelised across CPU cores using `multiprocessing.Pool` with worker-local tokenizer initialisation to avoid repeated model loading.

**Results on filtered local WET file (200-alpha-word threshold):**

| Metric | Value |
|--------|-------|
| Input documents | 7,143 |
| Total GPT-2 tokens | **13,308,651** |
| EOS tokens | 7,143 (one per doc ✓) |
| Max token ID | 50,256 (within uint16 range ✓) |
| Output file size | 26.6 MB |

Average document length: ~1,864 tokens.

For the full 5,000-WET filtered dataset (extrapolating from the ~30% keep rate): each WET file yields ~7,143 documents and ~13.3M tokens. Over 5,000 WET files this gives an estimated **~66.5 billion tokens** — far more than needed to train a GPT-2-small for 200K iterations at batch size 256 × 1024 tokens (~52B tokens consumed).

**CLI usage:**

```bash
# Local (single WET file already filtered)
uv run python tokenize_filtered_data.py \
    --input-glob "filtered_output/*.jsonl" \
    --output tokenized/train.bin

# Custom glob and worker count
uv run python tokenize_filtered_data.py \
    --input-glob "/data/filtered/*.jsonl" \
    --output /data/tokenized/train.bin \
    --workers 32
```

## Q2.1

### (a)

The extracted text contains significant boilerplate that should have been filtered out: navigation menus (e.g., "首页", "关于我们", "联系我们"), breadcrumb trails, phone numbers, copyright footers, and — most egregiously — injected adult spam keyword strings at the very top and bottom of the page that are entirely unrelated to the page's actual subject matter (industrial test equipment). Training on this kind of text risks teaching a model to associate unrelated concepts (e.g., pornographic keywords with laboratory instruments), to generate menu-like fragments as if they were prose, and to treat repeated contact info or navigational boilerplate as meaningful sentences. On the positive side, the structured product specification tables (dimensions, temperature ranges, power ratings) could be genuinely useful for a model learning Chinese technical vocabulary or understanding how to extract structured facts from semi-structured text.

### (c)

This example could be useful for training a Chinese-language model that needs to understand industrial/technical product descriptions, since the core content contains detailed equipment specifications. It would be harmful for a model intended for safe or child-appropriate text generation, since the spam keyword injections include explicit adult content that would corrupt the training signal.

