## Q2.2

### (b)

The resiliparse extractor produces noticeably cleaner output than the WET file for the same page (`0371rykj.com`): it correctly ignores the injected adult spam keyword strings that appear at the top and bottom of the WET version (those keywords were likely in hidden or visually-suppressed HTML elements that resiliparse skips), and it preserves structural formatting — navigation items become bullet-pointed lists with indentation, and headings are rendered distinctly. The WET file, by contrast, dumps everything as flat line-separated text, including the spam keywords, resulting in a noisier and more misleading document. Overall, the resiliparse extraction is better for training data purposes because it filters hidden/injected content and preserves semantic structure, though the WET version's flat-text format may be slightly simpler to work with downstream.

## Q2.3

### (b)

Errors in language identification can silently corrupt the language balance of training data: documents in language A may be mislabeled as language B and either retained when they should be filtered or vice versa, causing a model to under-represent certain languages or produce incoherent mixed-language outputs at inference time. Short documents, code-mixed text (e.g., Chinese navigation menus mixed with English URLs), and near-empty pages (error messages, login forms) are especially prone to misclassification. In a higher-stakes deployment, mitigations would include: (1) using an ensemble of language ID models and requiring agreement, (2) applying a high confidence threshold and sending borderline cases to human review, (3) running spot-checks on filtered-out data to measure false-negative rates, and (4) post-training evaluation on held-out per-language benchmarks to detect representation gaps before release.

