## Q2.4

### 4 (downstream problems)

Naïve PII masking introduces a new out-of-vocabulary token (`|||EMAIL_ADDRESS|||`, etc.) that the model must learn to handle, but because it appears so infrequently and in wildly varied contexts, the model may never learn its semantics properly — potentially generating the placeholder literally in completions. More subtly, aggressive masking can corrupt surrounding context: a sentence like "Email us at support@company.com for help" becomes "Email us at |||EMAIL_ADDRESS||| for help", which is fine, but a false positive on a version number like `3.0.2.1` masked as an IP turns coherent technical prose into nonsense. To mitigate these issues one could (1) only mask PII in high-stakes output contexts rather than training data, (2) replace with semantically similar synthetic values instead of a placeholder token, and (3) tune regex precision to minimize false positives rather than maximizing recall.

### 5 (false positives and false negatives from WARC scan)

**False positives observed:**
- `contact@domain.com` (`3rte.com.br`) — a placeholder/example email in an HTML form template, not real PII.
- `1234567890` and sequential numbers like `2627282930` matched as phone numbers — these are clearly dummy or sequential numeric strings, not real numbers.
- Chinese QQ account numbers (e.g., `1723913320`) double-matched: the QQ number appears both in `1723913320@qq.com` (correctly masked as email) and separately as a bare 10-digit string matched as a phone number.
- `642-001\n1064` (`acdcnpgalicia.es`) — the phone regex matched across a newline (since `\s` matches `\n`), stitching together two unrelated numbers from adjacent lines.

**False negatives likely:**
- International phone numbers with country codes (`+1-800-xxx-xxxx`, `+86-xxx-xxxxxxxxx`) are not matched since the regex only handles bare 10-digit or NPA-NXX-XXXX US formats.
- Emails with uncommon characters or internationalised domain names could be missed.
- Phone numbers formatted with dots (`818.841.9258`) are not captured.

## Q2.7

### (a)

**Approach: fastText binary classifier trained on Wikipedia reference text (positive) versus Common Crawl extractions (negative).**

*Training data construction.*  I used the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy article on Anarchism (`high_quality_wiki_reference.txt`) as the seed of positive (wiki) examples. The article was slid into overlapping chunks of 50, 100, and 200 whitespace-separated words, yielding 371 de-duplicated training paragraphs. For negative (cc) examples I extracted plain text from the first 400 HTML response records of a Common Crawl WARC segment (April 2025) using resiliparse, then split each document into 200-word chunks, yielding 2 273 cc examples. The resulting training file has the fastText `__label__wiki` / `__label__cc` prefix format.

*Model.* I trained a fastText supervised classifier with: word bi-grams (`wordNgrams=2`), embedding dimension 100, learning rate 0.5, 25 epochs, OvA loss. Training takes under 10 seconds on a laptop CPU. The model file is saved as `cs336_text_cleaning/quality_classifier.bin` (≈ 60 MB).

*Quality score.* The returned score is the raw softmax probability that fastText assigns to the top prediction; higher means more confident. A page is scored as `wiki` (high quality) or `cc` (low quality) together with this confidence.

*Limitations and improvements.* Because positive training data comes from a single encyclopaedia article, the classifier learns "dense academic English prose" as the wiki signal rather than a general notion of quality. In practice one would (1) collect thousands of Wikipedia-referenced URLs from the full dump, crawl them, and apply language/Gopher filtering to clean them; (2) sample CC negatives from the same crawl and time period so that domain-shift between positive and negative sets does not introduce spurious features; and (3) tune the decision threshold on a held-out labelled set rather than using the default argmax. The current model also has no multilingual capability — non-English high-quality pages would be incorrectly labelled as cc.

