The filter rejected 14/20 documents, which broadly aligns with my judgment given how many WARC pages consist of spam, empty error pages, or nav-heavy boilerplate. The most significant systematic error is that the mean-word-length check [3, 10] was designed for English and over-rejects Chinese content: Chinese whitespace-tokenised "words" (e.g. "内分泌资讯") average 5–20 characters, causing legitimate Chinese articles (records 12, 16) to fail even when they contain useful text. Conversely, purely structural pages like the Greek login form (records 10, 11) pass all checks because they happen to contain enough Greek prose in the navigation/header, illustrating that these filters do not distinguish navigation boilerplate from actual content. One notable content-quality blind spot: record 4 (adult video-chat platform) passes Gopher cleanly because it satisfies all length and ratio constraints; Gopher filters must therefore be combined with content-based classifiers rather than used in isolation.

## Q2.5

### 3 (downstream problems from harmful content filtering)

Because these classifiers were trained on English Wikipedia comments (the Jigsaw dataset), they are inherently language-biased: harmful content written in Chinese, Spanish, or other languages is largely invisible to them, which means non-English NSFW and toxic documents pass through the filter unchecked. Over-filtering is also a risk — an aggressive NSFW classifier may remove legitimate documents discussing sexual health, literature, or academic topics, creating a training corpus that under-represents these domains and causes a model to refuse or hedgehog on benign queries. To mitigate both issues one should (1) train or fine-tune separate classifiers for major non-English languages, (2) use domain-level signals (URL/domain blocklists) in conjunction with content classifiers, and (3) audit filtered-out examples to measure false-positive rates before committing to a threshold.

